  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nClouds cleared quickly across western and central Minnesota this \nmorning, which allowed temperatures to climb into the upper 60s to \nmid 70s before noon. Temperatures are now sitting in the upper 70s \nto low 80s just before 2pm. Further south and east, clouds and \nfog have been slower to clear out, but there should still be \nplenty of time for widespread highs in the mid to upper 70s for\nthis last day of September. A more seasonable airmass will \narrive following a frontal passage late tonight/early tomorrow. \nMost notably, Tds will tumble from the 60s today down into the \nupper 30s/40s. Northwest flow and CAA will remain in place into \nFriday before the surface high slides east and we see a slight \nbump in temperatures and moisture Saturday. A shortwave will \nmove along the International Border late in the day and may lead\nto a few isolated showers for mainly northern and central \nMinnesota.\n\nThe second half of the weekend, temperatures return to the low to \nmid 60s with ample sunshine. Clear skies and high pressure overhead \nwill make for what will likely be the chilliest night of the season \nso far for most. Monday morning lows are currently forecast to be in \nthe low 40s for western and southern Minnesota with mid 30s across \ncentral Minnesota into western Wisconsin. Following up on the \nclimate stats from the previous discussion, we typically drop below \n50 degrees for first time in the Twin Cities in late August. We have \nyet to do that this year, but it looks like Friday morning will \nchange that. With a current forecast of 42 degrees Monday morning, \nthis would be the coldest morning in the Twin Cities since May 20th \n(the forecasts for STC and EAU also point to it being the coldest \nmorning since mid May). This cool down will be brief with highs in \nthe upper 60s to low 70s returning by the end of the period.",
